<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp3sec3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

        <title>Injective Linear Maps and Independence</title>
        <proposition>
            <title><m>S</m> is independent iff the mixture map is injective</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>V</m> is a vector space over <m>F</m> and <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset V-\{\vect{0}\}</m>, then <m>S</m> is independent if and only if the mixture map <m>\Mix_S:F^n\to V</m> is injective.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. You will need to show both implications. First, suppose <m>S</m> is independent and suppose there exist <m>\vect{x},\vect{y}\in F^n</m> such that <m>\Mix_S(\vect{x})=\Mix_S(\vect{y})</m>. Analyze <m>\Mix_S(\vect{x})-\Mix_S(\vect{y})</m>. What can you then deduce? Next suppose <m>\Mix_S</m> is injective. Suppose there exists <m>x_1,\ldots x_n\in F</m> such that <m>x_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +x_n\vect{v}_n=\vect{0}</m>. Can you reformulate this in terms of <m>\Mix_S</m>? What can you then deduce? You might also find <xref ref="def-mixturemap"/>, <xref ref="def-dependence"/>, <xref ref="Def-inj_sur_bij"/>, <xref ref="def-linearmap"/>, <xref ref="def-vectorspace"/>, <xref ref="prop-mix_map_is_linear"/>, and <xref ref="prop_lin_map_zero"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition xml:id="prop_image_of_indpendent">
            <title>The image of an independent set is independent</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is an injective linear map and <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset V-\{\vect{0}\}</m> is a linearly independent set, then <m>T(S):=\{T(\vect{v}_1),\ldots, T(\vect{v}_n)\}\subset W</m> is a linearly independent set.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Suppose that there exists <m>x_1,\ldots, x_n\in F</m> such that <m>x_1T(\vect{v}_1)+\cdots+x_nT(\vect{v}_n)=\vect{0}_W</m>. What can you deduce about the <m>x_i</m>'s? You might also find <xref ref="def-dependence"/>, <xref ref="Def-inj_sur_bij"/>, <xref ref="def-linearmap"/>, <xref ref="def-vectorspace"/>, and <xref ref="prop_lin_map_zero"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-injective_kernel">
            <title>A map is injective iff its kernel is trivial</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>T:V\to W</m> is a linear map, then <m>T</m> is injective if and only if <m>\mathrm{ker}(T)=\{\vect{0}\}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. You will need to show both implications. Start by supposing <m>T</m> is injective. Pick <m>\vect{v}\in \ker (T)</m>. Deduce <m>\vect{v}=\vect{0}</m> (why???). Next suppose <m>\mathrm{ker}(T)=\{\vect{0}\}</m>. Pick <m>\vect{v},\vect{w}\in V</m> such that <m>T(\vect{v})=T(\vect{w})</m>. Analyze <m>T(\vect{v})-T(\vect{w})</m>. Deduce <m>\vect{v}=\vect{w}</m> (how???). You might also find <xref ref="def-dependence"/>, <xref ref="Def-inj_sur_bij"/>, <xref ref="def-linearmap"/>, and <xref ref="prop_lin_map_zero"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>


        <insight>
        <p>
            Let <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots,\vect{v}_n\} \subset V-\{\vect{0}\}</m>. Using the metaphor of mixtures, the domain <m>F^n</m> of the mixture map <m>\Mix_S</m> is the collection of all possible servings for our vectors in <m>S</m>. In the language of mixtures, and referencing the propositions above as appropriate, clearly explain how <m>\ker(\Mix_S)</m> encodes the dependency of <m>S</m>. Be sure to use proper mathematical language and notation. In what ways does this symbolism clarify your thinking? In what ways does it add confusion?
        </p>
        </insight>

</section>









